  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry conditions expected through the \nweekend with seasonable temperatures. \n\nThe center of the surface high pushes offshore today shifting winds \nto the east. With weak high pressure still in place, expect another \ndry day with mainly sunny skies. Temperatures will be in seasonable \nranges with highs in the low to mid 80s for the interior while \nonshore flow will keep the immediate coastal areas a little cooler \n(mid-upper 70s).\n\nBy Sunday, mid-level troughing arrives from west. Most guidance also \nindicates a low pressure system passing well offshore. There is \nstill a signal across guidance low level moisture to advect in from \noffshore within the easterly flow. Model soundings indicate a \nshallow layer of moisture at the surface with dry air above. It is \nmore likely that this layer will be too shallow to support any \nshowers/drizzle. A worst case would be some patchy drizzle for the \nouter Cape/Islands. There is some question on if the moisture makes \nit far enough west to impact our eastern-most CWA, so this is still \nmoderate confidence for now. Aside from the clouds in some areas, it \nwill be a mainly dry day with a northeasterly breeze for the eastern \ncoastal areas. Temperatures likely range in the low to mid 80s for \nthe interior and mid to upper 70s in the eastern and coastal\nareas. \n\nThere will be an increased risk for rip currents Sunday due to\nthe prolonged period of easterly component flow. The risk spans\nthe south and east coastline with the greatest risk for the \neast-facing beaches along the Cape and Islands and portions of\nthe east coast of MA.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periodic chances for showers and thunderstorms late \nMonday into Wednesday, potentially lingering into Thursday. Risk for \nlocalized heavy rainfall and flash flooding possible. \n\nA mid-level trough positions across the region through late-week. A \nseries of shortwaves embedded in the flow are expected to progress \nacross the region through the week supporting periodic chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms mainly in the late Mon-Wed timeframe. \nModel guidance is struggling to come into agreement with the details \nof the pattern mainly with regard to the speed/position of the main \ntrough and the potential for development of a closed low that tracks \nacross the northeast. If this low develops, there are solutions that \nindicate an anomalously strong low for late-July. There is a strong \nsignal for PWATs 120-170 percent of normal to advect into the region \nby Tuesday. If a low pressure develops, this would likely provide \nadditional forcing and increase the risk for convection with \nlocalized heavy downpours thus bring in a risk for localized flash \nflooding. This all comes down to the tracking of the features which \nwill dictate where the threat for the heavy showers/thunderstorms \nwill be the highest. It is possible the threat ends up west or south \nof the region or confined to western portions of southern New \nEngland. This is something we will need to keep watching as model \nguidance comes into better agreement over the next day or two. This \nwould likely fall in the late Monday through early Thursday \ntimeframe with most guidance favoring development of showers and \nthunderstorms in a more diurnal cadence (afternoon/evening) with the \nsupport of daytime heating/instability. \n\nHigh temperatures will likely hover between normal and slightly \nbelow normal for the week.",
